insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 1, 'Water injected into an oil reservoir does two jobs. What are they, and why does the distinction matter?', '["It holds pressure up and it cools the reservoir; both are measured by the voidage replacement ratio.", "It holds pressure up and it displaces oil along; the two are measured by different numbers and a flood can do one well while failing the other.", "It displaces oil and it dissolves gas back into solution; only the second is visible in a monthly ledger.", "It sweeps the pattern area and it sweeps the column; the voidage ledger reports the product of the two."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Voidage replacement measures the pressure job and only the pressure job. A flood at a flawless replacement ratio can still leave most of its oil behind if the water finds a fast path, which is why the displacement job needs its own measurements. Ekene is the case: a field cumulative near 1.03 that splits into two elements answering very different questions.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 2, 'Ekene''s design oil compressibility is 1.2e-5 per psi and its formation compressibility is 4e-6 per psi. What follows from numbers that small?', '["Pressure barely responds to production, so a reservoir can be depleted a long way before injection is needed.", "The tank expands so readily that voidage replacement has little effect on the pressure it reaches.", "The compressibilities can be neglected entirely in the material balance, which is why the ledger omits them.", "Recovering even a fraction of a percent of the oil in place costs hundreds of psi of reservoir pressure."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A small compressibility means the contents give up very little volume per psi, so a little production costs a great deal of pressure: Ekene spent 1104 psi to recover a little over two percent of its oil. That smallness is the entire reason waterflooding works, and the compressibilities are central to the balance rather than negligible in it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 3, 'In January 2023 Ekene produced 4727.034315745669 stb of oil and no water while injecting 4789.431168713511 bbl of water. What do the raw surface counts suggest, and what is true?', '["They suggest over-injection, while the properly converted ratio is 0.85, a deliberate fifteen percent under-injection.", "They suggest under-injection, and the properly converted ratio confirms it at 0.85 for the very first month of the ramp.", "They suggest balance, and the converted ratio of 1.05 shows the field was already at its long-run target.", "They are not comparable at all, since oil and water volumes cannot be added without a gas term."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Injection exceeds production on the raw counts, which reads as over-injection. Converting, the oil occupied 4727.034315745669 x 1.21584 = 5747.317402456214 rb and the water 4789.431168713511 x 1.02 = 4885.219792087782 rb, giving 0.85 exactly. Counting barrels at the surface got the SIGN of the answer wrong, which is what the whole voidage module exists to prevent.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 4, 'Why does the phrase ''voidage replacement ratio'' invite a misreading?', '["It sounds like a ratio of rates, when it is actually a ratio of cumulative volumes over the life of the flood.", "It sounds like a surface measurement, when every quantity in it is measured at reservoir conditions directly.", "It sounds like a recovery statement, when it is only a pressure statement.", "It sounds like a per-well quantity, when the engine can only ever compute it across the whole field at once."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A field can hold a replacement ratio of one for a decade while cycling water through a thief zone and recovering almost nothing extra. The number is necessary and nowhere near sufficient. It has instantaneous, rolling and cumulative forms, its inputs are surface volumes converted by formation volume factors, and it is perfectly computable per pattern once an allocation exists.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 5, 'Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 became the injectors. Why those two wells?', '["Both sit on the east side of the field, which gives the most regular five-spot geometry available.", "Both found the top of the sand below the 1560 m oil water contact, so they were wet and would never produce oil.", "Both had the highest measured injectivity index of the six wells, at 0.5 barrels per day per psi.", "Both were drilled after the flood was planned, so their completions were designed for water injection from the very start."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ekene-2 found the sand at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 1590 m, both below the 1560 m contact. A wet well in a sand you are about to flood is already completed in the right rock and converting it costs a pump and a line rather than a well. The eastern position is a consequence of that accident of geology rather than a design choice, and the injectivity index was an assumption until data existed.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 6, 'What does Ekene''s well arrangement illustrate about real flood patterns?', '["Real floods inherit whatever wells the drilling history left behind, and the pattern follows from that accident.", "Real floods are laid out as regular five-spots once the contact has been mapped and the wet wells identified.", "Real floods place injectors downdip of producers, which is why both Ekene injectors sit below the contact.", "Real floods convert producers to injectors only after primary depletion has taken the field below its bubble point."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Textbook floods are drawn as regular patterns on graph paper and real ones are assembled from the wells that already exist. Everything about Ekene''s behaviour, including the imbalance the Professional tier uncovers, follows from where the drilling history happened to put the two wet wells. Ekene''s flood in fact arrived while the reservoir was still 96 psi above its bubble point.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 7, 'At the flood start Ekene had produced 261475.03999967827 stb and its pressure had fallen from 3200 to 2096.0082626669955 psia, against a bubble point of 2000 psia. What does that say about the timing?', '["The flood arrived several years early, since 96 psi of margin is a comfortable operating buffer.", "The flood arrived late, since the reservoir had already dropped below its bubble point by that date.", "The flood arrived just before the reservoir would have started dissolving its own drive mechanism.", "The timing cannot be judged from pressure alone, since the bubble point depends on the flood-era gas cap."]'::jsonb, 2, '1104 psi was spent to recover a little over two percent of the oil in place, leaving 96 psi above the 2000 psia bubble point. Below that point gas comes out of solution, the oil viscosity rises, gas takes flow capacity from the oil, and the well starts producing the field''s energy instead of its oil. Ekene never went below it, which is also why its ledger can set Bg to zero.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 8, 'Ekene''s design fixes the injection split at 0.6 to Ekene-2 and 0.4 to Ekene-4 for the whole record. What is that split NOT?', '["A design input, since the split was derived from the two wells'' measured injectivity indices.", "A constant, since the split moves when Ekene-4''s injectivity degrades from 2025-01-01.", "Relevant to the ledger, since the field ledger sums both injectors before computing any ratio.", "A measurement of how much water each injector actually delivered to each of the producers."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The split says how the month''s target volume is divided between the two injection wells. Where that water then goes underground is the allocation question, which is judgement and belongs to the Professional tier. The split is a fixed design input rather than an injectivity-derived one, it does not move when the injectivity degrades, and it is exactly what the field ledger sums over.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 9, 'Ekene''s target profile is 0.85 + 0.04m for the first six months and then 1.05. Every month from the sixth onward hits 1.05 exactly. What does that exactness signal?', '["That the facility reached its design rate very quickly and then held it with unusual precision for three full years.", "That the case was constructed rather than measured, because the injection was computed FROM the produced voidage.", "That the ledger rounds its reported ratios to two decimal places once the ramp has finished.", "That the produced voidage was constant month to month, so a fixed injection volume gave a fixed ratio."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Real ledgers wobble. When a number lands exactly on a round design value, the case was built rather than observed, and recognising that is part of reading any teaching field honestly. Ekene''s produced voidage does vary month to month; the ratio stays fixed because the injection volume was derived from it each month rather than set independently.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 10, 'Ekene-6 is 715.8910531638176 m from Ekene-2 and 989.9494936611666 m from Ekene-4. What standing does that give a distance-based allocation?', '["It is a reasonable starting hypothesis and remains a judgement, which two independent diagnostics later fail to confirm.", "It is a measurement of connectivity, since transit time scales with distance in a homogeneous sand.", "It is irrelevant, since allocation in the Petrolord engine is derived from the injection split instead.", "It is conclusive for the nearest producer, though ambiguous for producers roughly equidistant from both."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Distance is a hypothesis, not a measurement. It is roughly what the Professional tier''s allocation matrix uses, and the course then shows two diagnostics that decline to confirm it. Connectivity depends on the rock between the wells rather than on the separation, and the allocation matrix is a separate input from the injection split.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 11, 'The surveillance file holds daily rates and the ledger file holds monthly volumes for the same flood. What relates them?', '["A conversion through the formation volume factors, since rates are surface and volumes are reservoir.", "An aggregation over wells, since the rate file is per well and the volume file is per field period.", "A single multiplication by the number of days in the month.", "A calendar alignment, since the rate rows are dated daily and the volume rows are dated monthly."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Volume equals rate times days in the month and there is no other difference between the files. Both are surface quantities, both are one row per well per month, and running buildFieldPeriods over the 216 per-well ledger rows reproduces the committed field periods to a maximum relative difference of 3.9364202640579826e-16, which is floating point noise.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 12, 'Read as monthly volumes Ekene''s cumulative VRR is 1.034899536109; read as 36 equal-weight rows it is 1.034709324454895. Why is the gap only 0.018379721651073933 percent here?', '["Because the two calculations differ only in rounding, and the underlying volumes are identical.", "Because Ekene''s injection was computed from its production each month, so the ratio barely varies with month length.", "Because February''s shortfall is offset by the 31-day months on either side of it over a full year.", "Because the engine already normalises every period to a mean month of 30.4375 days before aggregating."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Treating each row as one equal-weight sample gives February the same weight as January, and the error is worth however much the ratio you care about correlates with month length. On Ekene the ratio is nearly constant, so the error is tiny; on a field whose injection is set by a facility constraint it can be worth several percent. Neither number is wrong and a report must say which it used.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 13, 'The engine derives a row''s month by string arithmetic rather than by parsing the date into a date object. What does that avoid?', '["A timezone shift moving a row dated the first of the month into the previous month, silently.", "A locale difference reading the day and the month fields of the date in the opposite order entirely.", "A leap year adding a day to February and changing the divisor used in the rate conversion.", "A daylight saving transition making one month an hour shorter than the calendar says."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Parsing to a date object and asking for its month works until a timezone gets involved, at which point a row dated the first in one zone becomes the last day of the previous month in another and a volume moves between periods with no error raised. monthKeyOf works on the leading characters of the string, so ''2023-05-17'' and ''2023-05'' both give ''2023-05'' and ''May 2023'' gives null.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 14, 'Ekene-1 produced oil at 32.210476049246076 stb/d in January 2023. What is the month''s volume, and what makes the arithmetic worth doing slowly?', '["980.4063647489274 stb, using the mean month of 30.4375 days that the forecast engine also uses.", "998.5247575266284 stb, and the point is that the rate had to be converted to reservoir barrels first.", "998.5247575266284 stb, and the point is that the multiplier is the month''s own length rather than a constant.", "966.3142814773822 stb, using a nominal 30-day month rather than January''s own length."]'::jsonb, 2, 'January has 31 days, so 32.210476049246076 x 31 = 998.5247575266284 stb. Using a mean month here would be the equal-weight error the lesson warns about. The conversion to reservoir barrels is a separate step that happens later in the voidage equation, and there is no mid-month correction: the rate is the month''s average by construction.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-what-a-waterflood-is', 15, 'What does this course explicitly NOT do, by design?', '["Compute voidage at reservoir conditions, which the Material Balance course owns and this course imports.", "Analyse per-pattern behaviour, which is left entirely to a reservoir simulator rather than to any course.", "Use the Ekene field, since the flood-era data is synthetic and the course works from published cases instead.", "Re-derive displacement physics, which the SCAL course owns and this course imports."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Displacement physics, the Corey curves, fractional flow and the Welge tangent belong to the SCAL and Displacement course, and RC4 takes their answers rather than reconstructing them. Volumetrics stay with ReservoirCalc and valuation with Economics. Voidage at reservoir conditions is precisely what this course does own, and pattern analysis is the Professional tier of this same course.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 1, 'Why is the oil formation volume factor greater than one for a live oil?', '["Because the reservoir is at higher pressure, which compresses the oil into a smaller volume than the tank.", "Because water and sediment are stripped out at the separator.", "Dissolved gas leaves the oil on the way to the tank and the oil cools, and both shrink the parcel.", "Because the factor is defined as tank barrels over reservoir barrels, which inverts for any live oil sample."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both effects act in the same direction: the gas that was dissolved at reservoir conditions leaves and takes its volume with it, and the hot reservoir oil cools on the way up. Ekene''s 1.21584 says a stock tank barrel occupied nearly one and a quarter barrels of pore space. Compression would make the reservoir volume smaller rather than larger, and the ratio is reservoir over stock tank.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 2, 'Ignoring formation volume factors always makes a flood look better than it is. What makes the direction of that error predictable rather than arbitrary?', '["Bo exceeds Bw, so produced oil is under-counted more than injected water is, and the ratio of injection to production comes out too high.", "The injected volume is metered continuously while produced oil is sampled, so injection carries the smaller error.", "Produced water is omitted entirely from raw surface counts.", "Gas voidage is left out of the numerator, which is where the injected fluids sit, so the ratio rises."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s factors are 1.21584 for oil against 1.02 for water, so leaving them out shrinks the denominator far more than the numerator and the VRR is overstated. January 2023 is the demonstration: raw counts read as over-injection where the truth was a deliberate 0.85. Metering practice and gas voidage are not what sets the sign.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 3, 'Handing the daily surveillance engine a factor object with the wrong key names moves Ekene''s cumulative VRR from 1.034709324454895 to 1.2263567352896008. Why is that failure especially dangerous?', '["The engine raises a warning that most callers suppress.", "The engine falls back to factors of 1 with no error or warning, and a flood reading 1.23 looks comfortably over-injected.", "The inflated value exceeds the over-injection band, so the dashboard flags it and the real cause is masked by the alert.", "The factor of 1.185218598407499 is close enough to unity that the difference falls inside the reporting tolerance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nineteen percent, silently: no error is raised, nothing is null, and the resulting number is entirely plausible. An implausible number gets checked and a plausible wrong one gets reported, which is why the silence rather than the size is the danger. The engine reads lowercase bo, bw, bg and rs, and the capitalised ledger set defaults Bo to 1.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 4, 'Ekene''s ledger freezes its factors at 2100 psia. What does the PVT line Bo(p) = 1.2(1 + 1.2e-5(3200 - p)) give there?', '["1.2, since the line is referenced to the initial pressure of 3200 psia and 2100 psia lies below the bubble point.", "1.0132, which is the bracketed expansion term rather than the factor itself.", "1.19712, which is the value the interpolator clamps to above the top of the table.", "1.21584"]'::jsonb, 3, '1.2 x (1 + 1.2e-5 x 1100) = 1.2 x 1.0132 = 1.21584. At the initial 3200 psia the same line gives 1.2, which is the Boi the volumetric booking used, and 1.19712 is what the interpolator returns when clamped at 9000 psia. Freezing is a convention rather than a law, and module 5 measures what it was worth.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 5, 'In the voidage equation, where does produced water belong and why?', '["In the denominator alongside the oil, because produced water is volume that has left the pore space.", "In the numerator with the injected water, since the equation is fundamentally a balance on the water phase.", "In neither, since produced water is re-injected and therefore cancels out of the ratio entirely.", "In the numerator, but reduced by the fraction that was formation water rather than injected water returning."]'::jsonb, 0, 'VRR is a VOIDAGE balance, not a water balance: what left the pore space against what was put back. Produced water left, so it belongs with the oil in the denominator, and only injected fluids appear on top. That is why a well starting to make water drives the VRR down and forces injection up to hold a target, which is how mature floods come to inject enormous volumes for modest oil.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 6, 'Ekene''s January 2023 has Gp = 1890.8137262982677 Mscf and Rs Np/1000 = 1890.8137262982677 Mscf. What does that equality say about the reservoir?', '["That the gas meter and the oil meter were calibrated against each other, which is standard practice.", "That the solution gas ratio was back-calculated from the gas production.", "Every molecule at the gas meter had been dissolved in oil, so no free gas ever existed in the rock.", "That the reservoir sat exactly at its bubble point through the month, so gas came out of solution at the same rate as it was produced."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ekene stayed above its 2000 psia bubble point through primary and the flood arrived before it dropped through, so no gas ever came out of solution in the rock and there was never free gas to produce. That is a statement about the reservoir rather than about metering, and it is why the ledger can set Bg to zero without losing anything.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 7, 'What is the max(0, ...) doing in Gfree = max(0, Gp - Rs Np / 1000)?', '["Selecting the larger of the metered gas and the solution gas, so that neither source is under-counted.", "Keeping a small negative rounding in either of the two terms from becoming a negative voidage.", "Enforcing the physical rule that produced gas can never be less than the gas dissolved in the produced oil.", "Preventing a division by zero when the solution gas ratio Rs is itself zero in the factor set."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The subtraction can land a hair below zero on real data through rounding in either term, and a negative free gas would subtract voidage that never left. The clamp is arithmetic hygiene, not a physical claim: produced gas genuinely can be less than the solution gas, for instance when gas is being re-injected or when the allocation lags. Rs appearing as zero is a convention choice rather than a division.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 8, 'Reading Ekene''s January 2023 with Bg = 0.9 rb/Mscf and Rs = 0 gives a produced voidage of 7449.049756124655 rb and a VRR of 0.6558178495278708. What has changed?', '["The gas measurement, since a non-zero Bg requires the gas to be re-metered at reservoir conditions.", "The oil formation volume factor, which must be reduced once the solution gas is counted separately.", "The period definition, since a free-gas run aggregates on a different month boundary than a solution-gas run.", "The convention, not the data."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Same volumes, same month, different PVT convention: setting Rs to zero removes the subtraction and every one of the 1890.8137262982677 Mscf is charged 0.9 reservoir barrels. The answer moves from 0.85 to 0.66, a 23 percent error, purely from a convention the reader cannot see in the output. That is the case for quoting conventions alongside the number.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 9, 'Three conventions are baked into any quoted VRR and none is discoverable from the answer. What are they?', '["The pressure the factors were evaluated at, whether gas voidage is counted at all, and whether the solution gas subtraction was applied.", "The window length, the target band, and whether the series is instantaneous or cumulative.", "The well classification rule, the month key convention, and the duplicate handling policy.", "The allocation matrix, the pattern definitions, and the treatment of out-of-zone injection."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those three are the first questions to ask when inheriting someone else''s flood ledger, because a VRR quoted without them is not reproducible. The window and the series type are reporting choices visible in the label, and the allocation and pattern questions belong to the Professional tier rather than to the voidage equation itself.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 10, 'What kind of quantity does Bg convert, and how does that differ from Bo and Bw?', '["A reservoir gas volume into the equivalent volume of oil it would have been dissolved in at the bubble point.", "A produced gas volume into a solution gas ratio.", "A gas volume measured at standard conditions into the space it occupies at reservoir pressure.", "A free gas volume into a total gas volume, by adding back the gas still dissolved in the produced oil at tank conditions."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Bo and Bw both relate a surface liquid volume to the space that same parcel occupied in the reservoir. Bg does the analogous job for gas but the parcel is enormously more compressible, so Bg is a small number in reservoir barrels per thousand standard cubic feet and it moves strongly with pressure. Ekene''s ledger uses zero because there is no free gas for it to multiply.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 11, 'A field produces oil and water and injects water. Why can no consistent surface accounting make the formation volume factors cancel out of the VRR?', '["Because the factors change with pressure, so any fixed convention drifts apart over the life of the flood.", "The numerator and denominator carry different fluids with different factors, so there is nothing to cancel.", "Because injected water is metered cold while produced water is metered hot.", "Because the numerator carries a gas term whose factor has different units from the liquid factors, so the ratio is dimensionally mixed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Injected water is multiplied by 1.02 and produced oil by 1.21584, so the two sides are scaled by different numbers and no consistency adopted at the surface makes them the same. Pressure dependence and metering conditions are real issues of their own, but even with perfectly constant factors and identical metering the cancellation still fails.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 12, 'Ekene''s last month, 2025-12, produced 3670.7384235169648 stb of oil and 962.9824550781937 bbl of water while injecting 5605.427788705937 bbl. What does the water do to the ratio?', '["It raises the produced voidage to 5445.272709028624 rb, so more injection is needed to hold the same target.", "It raises the injected voidage, since produced water is routed back into the injectors.", "It lowers the produced voidage, because water occupies less reservoir space per barrel than oil does.", "It leaves the ratio unchanged, since water appears on both sides of the equation and its factor is the same."]'::jsonb, 0, '3670.7384235169648 x 1.21584 + 962.9824550781937 x 1.02 = 5445.272709028624 rb produced against 5717.536344480056 rb injected, giving 1.05. Produced water sits only in the denominator, so it pushes the ratio down and injection has to climb to compensate. Water does occupy less space per barrel than oil, but it adds volume rather than removing it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 13, 'Setting the ledger explorer''s rolling window to 1 puts the rolling line exactly on the instantaneous line. Why is that worth doing once?', '["It calibrates the panel against the engine, since the two are computed by different code paths.", "It reveals the float tails in the design arithmetic.", "It shows that a one-period trailing average is the period itself, so the window stops being magic.", "It confirms the cumulative line is the limiting case of the rolling line, which only holds at a window of 1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A trailing average over one period is a sum of one term divided by a sum of one term, which is the ratio itself, and seeing the two lines coincide makes that concrete. The cumulative line is the OTHER limit, reached when the window is at least as long as the record. The panel and the engine share the same computation rather than duplicating it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 14, 'Ekene''s ledger uses Bw = 1.02, close to one. Why is the water factor so much nearer unity than the oil factor?', '["Water is metered at the injection pump, which is already close to reservoir conditions.", "Formation water carries very little dissolved gas and has a low compressibility, so it barely shrinks on the way up.", "Water is incompressible by definition in the material balance, so its factor is set to unity plus a thermal term.", "The water factor describes injected water rather than formation water, and injected water has no dissolved gas at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The two mechanisms that inflate Bo are dissolved gas leaving and thermal contraction, and water has almost no dissolved gas to lose, so only the smaller thermal effect remains. Water is not treated as strictly incompressible in the balance, where its compressibility enters the effective term, and the factor applies to reservoir water volume regardless of origin.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-voidage', 15, 'January 2023''s produced voidage is 4727.034315745669 x 1.21584 = 5747.317402456214 rb, with no water and no free gas. What is the injected voidage?', '["4789.431168713511 rb, which is the injected water volume itself, since injected water needs no conversion.", "5747.317402456214 rb, since the design target for the month was voidage replacement.", "4695.520753640697 rb, being the injected volume divided rather than multiplied by the water factor.", "4885.219792087782 rb"]'::jsonb, 3, '4789.431168713511 x 1.02 = 4885.219792087782 rb, and 4885.219792087782 / 5747.317402456214 = 0.85 exactly, the designed first-month target. Injected water needs the same conversion as produced water, the target that month was 0.85 rather than 1.0, and the factor multiplies rather than divides going from surface to reservoir.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 1, 'The rolling VRR is described as a ratio of sums rather than a mean of ratios. Why does the distinction matter?', '["It does not matter in practice, since the two agree whenever the window is at least three periods long.", "Summing the voidages first weights each month by how much voidage it actually created.", "A mean of ratios is undefined in any month with zero produced voidage.", "The ratio of sums is the only form that reduces to the instantaneous series at a window of one, which the mean of ratios does not."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On a field where produced voidage varies month to month the two are different numbers, and the ratio of sums is correct because a month that created twice the voidage should count twice. Both forms reduce to the instantaneous value at a window of one, and both have trouble with a zero denominator, so neither of those is what separates them.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 2, 'Ekene''s instantaneous series prints its third value as 0.9299999999999999 and its fourth as 0.9700000000000001. What are those tails?', '["Measurement noise in the allocation meters, which is why the ledger reports to sixteen figures rather than rounding.", "Accumulated rounding from the monthly aggregation of 216 per-well rows into 36 field periods.", "The difference between the frozen and the pressure-tracked formation volume factors, showing up in the last digits.", "The exact binary result of 0.85 + 0.04m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The design arithmetic is 0.85 + 0.04m and neither 0.04 nor its multiples are exactly representable in binary, so the sixteenth digit moves. A number that differs from a round value in the sixteenth digit IS that round value, and learning not to chase those is the point of printing them. The fixture carries no measurement noise at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 3, 'Which of the three VRR forms connects most directly to reservoir pressure, and why?', '["The cumulative, because pressure responds to accumulated net withdrawal rather than to this month''s.", "The instantaneous, because pressure is a state variable and responds to the current rate of withdrawal.", "The rolling, because its window can be tuned to the reservoir''s pressure response time.", "All three equally, since they are the same ratio evaluated over different spans."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The material balance relates pressure change to CUMULATIVE net withdrawal, so the cumulative VRR is the form the balance speaks to. It also has the longest memory: a bad first year is still in the number three years later, which is a feature or an annoyance depending on the question. Pressure does not respond to an instantaneous rate in a tank model.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 4, 'Ekene''s cumulative VRR series crosses 1.0 for the first time at period index 11. What is that crossing called and what does it mark?', '["Breakthrough, the month at which injected water first reaches a producing well.", "The target crossing, the month at which the design profile reaches 1.05.", "Fill-up: the month at which cumulative injection has replaced everything taken since the record began.", "The pressure break-even, the month at which the tank stops losing pressure and starts gaining it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'findFillUp reports index 11, label 2023-12, startedAbove false, at a cumulative of 1.0040017280735174. Breakthrough is a displacement event at a producer and happened at Ekene-6 in March 2024. The target reaches 1.05 in month index 5, and the pressure trough sits at index 3 because break-even is 0.9869719699960521 rather than 1.0.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 5, 'The Ekene flood deliberately under-injects for its first five months. Which set of reasons is the standard justification?', '["Injected water must first fill the free gas saturation before it can displace any oil.", "Facilities are still commissioning, injectivity is not yet measured, and the reservoir has margin it does not need spending.", "The formation parting pressure is lowest at start-up and rises as the flood establishes a pressure gradient.", "Early injection would arrive at the producers before the oil bank has formed, so the water would be recycled."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A new water plant does not reach design rate on day one, an injectivity index is an estimate until a well has been injected into, and Ekene sat 96 psi above its bubble point with no urgency. Writing a target the facility cannot deliver produces a report full of red flags describing normal commissioning. Ekene has no free gas at all, and parting pressure does not behave that way.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 6, 'Ekene holds a target of 1.05 rather than 1.0 for 31 months. What is the extra five percent buying, and at what cost?', '["Slow repressurization, ending 34.4931292839633 psi above the trough, paid for in water sourcing, treating, pumping and disposal.", "Insurance against measurement error in the allocation meters, at the cost of a slightly noisier ratio.", "A margin against produced water, which will otherwise pull the ratio below 1.0 as the water cut climbs.", "Faster breakthrough at the producers, which brings the displacement forward at the cost of earlier water handling."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Injecting more voidage than you remove means net withdrawal is falling, so pressure rises. Thirty five psi is modest and it is thirty five psi of extra margin above the bubble point bought without drilling anything. The three reasons not to aim higher are the cost of the water, the fracture pressure ceiling, and the fact that over-injected water may simply be recycled.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 7, 'A field is running at a VRR of 0.9. Is it failing?', '["Yes, since 0.9 is the lower bound of the generic interpretation band and anything at or below it is under-injection.", "No, since a cumulative VRR of 0.9 still sits above the pressure break-even for a typical undersaturated oil.", "Yes, unless the field is above its bubble point, in which case the deficit can be recovered later at no cost.", "It depends entirely on its target profile."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A field at 0.9 is failing against a target of 1.05 and comfortably on plan against a target of 0.9, so the target profile is the first thing to ask for when taking over a flood. classifyVRR''s generic bands at 0.9 and 1.1 are reservoir engineering; the operator''s target band is a field-specific input you supply, and confusing the two produces a dashboard that flags the plan as a problem.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 8, 'In row 8 of the Ekene window table the field has injected at 1.05 for three consecutive months, yet the twelve-period rolling VRR reads 0.9775428385647656. What is that number?', '["An error introduced by the partial-window convention at the start of the record.", "A stale value, since the engine only refreshes the long-window series annually.", "A correct statement about the last twelve months, four of which were genuinely below target.", "A weighted blend of the cumulative and instantaneous series, which the engine uses once the window exceeds the record length so far."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing is wrong with it. It is a correct answer to a question about the last twelve months and a badly misleading answer to how the flood is doing now. If it is the number on the dashboard, the field will not appear to reach target until the ramp falls out of the window, four months after it actually happened. Match the window to the question and put the question in the axis label.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 9, 'A trailing window of n periods introduces roughly what delay for a steadily changing signal, and what does that imply?', '["About n periods, so the window length is the delay and a three-month window lags by three months.", "About (n - 1)/2 periods, so a twelve-month window cannot see a two-month failure mode in time.", "None, since a trailing window is causal.", "About the square root of n periods, which is why increasing the window has diminishing effect on both the smoothing and the lag."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At n = 3 the delay is one month and at n = 12 it is five and a half. A causal window still lags: it is an average over the past, so it reports where the signal has been rather than where it is. Smoothing and lag are the same effect rather than two, which is why there is no setting that is both fast and stable.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 10, 'The engine''s convention at the start of a record is to use whatever periods exist rather than returning null until the window is full. What visible consequence does that have?', '["The n = 6 and n = 12 columns are identical for the first six periods, because neither has a full window yet.", "The first period of every series is null regardless of window length.", "Long-window series start above the instantaneous series and converge downward onto it.", "The cumulative series is undefined until the longest configured window has been filled."]'::jsonb, 0, 'With partial windows both long-window columns are simply the running average of everything so far until they differ in length, so they coincide for the first six periods. The alternative convention of returning null until full is also defensible and some tools use it, so knowing which one you are looking at is a prerequisite to reading anything into the first year of a chart.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 11, 'Ekene ends its record at cumulative 1.034899536109, latest instantaneous 1.05 and three-period rolling 1.05. What do the three together say that none says alone?', '["The field has replaced its voidage exactly and its pressure has returned to the initial value.", "The rolling and instantaneous agree, so the record is noise-free.", "The field is on target now, has been long enough for a three-month average to agree, and carries a small historical surplus.", "The cumulative is below the instantaneous, which means the flood is still recovering from an early deficit and has not yet reached its design target."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That is a complete pressure story in three numbers, and quoting one of them alone leaves the reader guessing which. The surplus came from the period after start-up when the field injected 1.05 against an early deficit of 0.85. Pressure has not returned to the initial value, and the field reached its 1.05 target in month five.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 12, 'Why is it wrong to read a cumulative VRR of 1.0 as meaning the pressure came back to where it started?', '["Because pressure also responds to aquifer influx, which the cumulative VRR cannot see.", "The VRR uses factors at a chosen convention while the tank measures expansion from its own initial state, and for Ekene break-even is 0.9869719699960521.", "Because the cumulative VRR is a surface ratio.", "Because the pressure at the end of a record is an average over the field, while the VRR is computed from well-level allocations that are not spatially weighted."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The ledger books a produced barrel at Bo evaluated at 2100 psia while the tank measures expansion from Boi at 3200 psia, and those references differ by 1.3 percent of volume. So break-even sits at Boi/Bo rather than at 1. Aquifer influx and spatial averaging are real effects, but the gap here is a convention rather than either of them, and the VRR is computed in reservoir barrels throughout.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 13, 'Ekene''s cumulative totals are 229474.93559224083 rb injected against 221736.43680913927 rb produced. What is the net, and what does it mean physically?', '["A surplus of 7738.498783101561 reservoir barrels, which is volume put back beyond what was taken and therefore pressure support.", "A deficit of 7738.498783101561 reservoir barrels, which the field must still make up.", "A surplus of 7738.498783101561 stock tank barrels of displaced oil.", "A balance, since the two totals agree to within the 0.018379721651073933 percent that separates the volume and rate readings of the same record."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Injection exceeds production by 7738.498783101561 rb, which is a surplus rather than a deficit, and at roughly 288 reservoir barrels of tank expansion per psi that surplus is worth tens of psi of repressurization. Both totals are voidage in reservoir barrels rather than displaced oil, and the gap is far larger than the volume-versus-rate discrepancy.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 14, 'What are the two exact limits of the rolling window that are worth knowing?', '["A window of 1 reproduces the cumulative series, and an infinite window reproduces the instantaneous.", "A window of 3 is the operational optimum.", "A window of 1 reproduces the instantaneous series, and a window at least as long as the record reproduces the cumulative.", "A window equal to the pressure survey interval reproduces the pressure track, which is why six is the natural choice on this field."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Those two limits bracket everything else the rolling series can do, and both are exact rather than approximate. There is no universal optimum window: a short one reacts fast and is noisy, a long one is stable and slow, and choosing is a decision about which error you would rather make. The survey interval has nothing to do with the rolling window.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-reading-vrr', 15, 'Which VRR form belongs on an operational dashboard, and why?', '["The instantaneous, because it is the only form that responds immediately to a change in injection rate.", "The cumulative, because it has the longest memory and therefore the least month-to-month noise.", "All three, because a dashboard that shows fewer than three is by definition incomplete and will mislead its reader.", "The rolling, as a compromise between noise and lag."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Month-to-month VRR on a real field is genuinely noisy and the cumulative is too slow to react, so the rolling form is the operational number. That is a statement about which single number belongs on a dashboard; the three-number report is what belongs in a written summary, where there is room to say which is which.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 1, 'Ekene''s effective compressibility term is cefw = (Swi cw + cf)/(1 - Swi) = (0.35 x 3e-6 + 4e-6)/0.65. What is that expression doing?', '["Expressing the connate water and rock contributions per unit of HYDROCARBON pore volume, which is what the tank equation is written against.", "Averaging the water and rock compressibilities by their volume fractions in the pore space.", "Converting the rock compressibility from a pore-volume basis to a bulk-volume basis.", "Removing the water saturation."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tank equation multiplies by N Boi, which is the oil volume, so the water and rock terms have to be rescaled from total pore volume onto that basis, and dividing by 1 - Swi is that rescaling. The result is 7.76923076923077e-6 per psi, and adding the oil compressibility gives a total of 1.976923076923077e-5 per psi.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 2, 'Ekene''s tank gives about 287.97936771938475 reservoir barrels of expansion per psi. What is the practical reading of that?', '["The reservoir can absorb 288 rb of injection per psi of increase in the wellhead injection pressure.", "Each psi of drawdown at a producer mobilises 288 rb of oil from the surrounding rock.", "Removing about 288 rb without replacing it costs one psi of reservoir pressure.", "Reaching the bubble point 96 psi below the flood-start pressure requires producing about 288 x 96 stock tank barrels of oil, which sets the remaining primary reserve."]'::jsonb, 2, 'N Boi (co + cefw) is the reservoir volume the tank contents give up per psi of pressure drop, so its inverse is the psi cost of unreplaced voidage. It says nothing about wellhead pressure or drawdown, both of which are near-well flow quantities rather than tank properties, and the figure is in reservoir barrels of voidage rather than stock tank oil.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 3, 'The flood-era pressure inversion is Dp = (Np + W)/(N(co + cefw) - Np co) with W = (Wp - Wi) Bw / Boi. How does injection enter?', '["As an additional positive term in the denominator, which increases the tank''s effective compressibility.", "As a negative withdrawal, so the flood-era track continues the depletion-era track with no seam or refitting.", "Through a separate injection-support term added after the depletion solution is evaluated.", "By resetting the reference pressure to the value at the flood start, after which the depletion form applies again with a new initial condition."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Setting W to zero recovers exactly the depletion-era inversion from the Material Balance course, so nothing about the model changes when the flood starts. The check is the seam: at the flood start the inversion returns 2096.0082626669955 psia, which is exactly what the RC2 fixture reports, because both courses run the same closed form.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 4, 'Where does Ekene''s flood-era pressure track reach its minimum?', '["2096.0082626669955 psia at the flood start, since the flood begins supporting pressure immediately.", "2091.380075337029 psia at 2023-07, which is where the six-monthly survey series bottoms out.", "2123.4461408278908 psia at 2025-12, which is the end of the record and therefore the lowest point of a monotonically falling track.", "2088.9530115439275 psia at 2023-04."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The shape is a shallow V: pressure falls for the first few months while the flood is ramping below break-even, bottoms at 2023-04, and rises for the remaining 32 months. 2091.380075337029 at 2023-07 is where the INTERPOLATED SURVEY series bottoms out, three months late and 2.427063793101752 psi high, and 2123.4461408278908 is the highest point rather than the lowest.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 5, 'Ekene''s pressure break-even is Boi/Bo = 1.2/1.21584 = 0.9869719699960521 rather than 1.0. What causes the gap?', '["The ledger books produced oil at Bo evaluated at 2100 psia while the tank measures expansion from Boi at the initial 3200 psia, and 1100 psi of oil expansion is 1.3 percent of volume.", "The 1.02 water factor, which makes injected volume slightly cheaper than produced volume in reservoir barrels.", "The rock compressibility.", "The solution gas subtraction, which removes gas voidage from the denominator and so lowers the ratio at which the two sides balance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ledger asks how much reservoir space this month''s production occupied at flood-era conditions and the tank asks how much the contents have expanded relative to their INITIAL state, and the two references differ by 1100 psi. So a barrel booked as 1.21584 rb of voidage is only 1.2 rb of demand on the tank''s expansion budget. Neither is wrong, and the 1.3 percent is the price of asking them separately.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 6, 'Ekene''s target profile crosses 1.0 between month index 3 (target 0.97) and index 4 (target 1.01), yet the pressure trough sits at index 3. What does that show?', '["That the pressure track lags the injection by one month, which is the tank''s response time.", "That the trough is quantised to the survey cadence.", "That break-even really is below 1.0, since the 0.97 month was already above 0.9869719699960521.", "That the ledger''s month indexing is zero-based while the pressure track is one-based, so the two series are offset by one period."]'::jsonb, 2, 'If break-even were 1.0 the trough would sit at index 4, where the target first exceeds one. It sits at index 3 because a target of 0.97 already exceeds 0.9869719699960521, so pressure had started rising by then. That is a convention choice showing up as the position of a minimum, and it would be very easy to mistake for a small error in the data.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 7, 'The simple estimate 7738.498783101561 rb / 287.97936771938475 rb per psi gives 26.87171252713553 psi, while the quoted recovery is 34.4931292839633 psi. Are they inconsistent?', '["Yes: the simple estimate omits the -Np co term, which accounts for the whole 7.6 psi difference.", "No: 26.87 psi is the rise from the FLOOD START, which the track puts at 27.437878160895252 psi, and 34.49 psi is measured from the trough.", "No: the two differ only by rounding.", "Yes: the surplus should be divided by the tank expansion evaluated at the trough pressure rather than at the initial pressure, which is what makes the simple estimate low."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two correct numbers can disagree because they are measured from different origins, and the fix is to say which origin. The trough sits 7.055251123068047 psi below the flood start because the flood spent its first four months still losing ground. The -Np co term is real but worth only about two percent on Ekene, not 7.6 psi.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 8, 'What does the -Np co term in the inversion''s denominator do, and when does it matter?', '["It makes the tank slightly more responsive to withdrawal as cumulative production grows, worth about two percent on Ekene and much more on a field that has produced a large fraction of its oil.", "It corrects for the oil formation volume factor changing with pressure, which is otherwise held frozen.", "It removes the produced oil from the tank volume.", "It accounts for the produced water, which is not otherwise represented in the denominator of the inversion."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The denominator shrinks as Np grows, so a given withdrawal costs more pressure later in life than earlier. On Ekene''s small cumulative production that is worth about two percent, which is exactly the gap between the simple estimate and the track. On a mature field it is worth far more, and a linear rule of thumb in reservoir barrels per psi stops working.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 9, 'Ekene''s record carries six flood-era pressure surveys in 36 months. Why so few?', '["Because the tank pressure is an average, so additional surveys in the same well add no independent information.", "Because the pressure gauge resolution of 5 psi makes more frequent surveys indistinguishable from one another.", "Because surveys are only meaningful once the pressure has moved by more than the spatial variation across the field, which takes about six months.", "A survey means shutting a well in for days."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pressure is measured by shutting a well in and letting it build up, which costs production and takes days, so nobody does it monthly. That is precisely why voidage is worth having: it is computed from meters that run anyway, it exists at monthly resolution, and it costs nothing extra, provided you keep checking it against the measurement whenever the measurement arrives.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 10, 'The six-monthly survey cadence puts the apparent pressure minimum at 2023-07 rather than the true 2023-04. Can better interpolation fix that?', '["Yes, a spline through the six surveys recovers the curvature that linear interpolation discards.", "Yes, provided the model track is used to constrain the interpolation between surveys.", "No: the segment between the February and August surveys is monotone while the truth is V-shaped.", "No, but the error can be removed by shifting every survey back by three months, since each survey reads the end of the previous month rather than the date it carries."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A six-monthly cadence cannot resolve a two-month pressure event: there is simply no data inside the feature, and no interpolation scheme invents information that was never sampled. Using the model track to constrain the interpolation would mean the survey series no longer tests the model. The half-period shift is a real convention but it is not what hides the trough.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 11, 'A field holds a cumulative VRR of 1.00 for two years and its pressure falls 200 psi. What is the most likely reading?', '["That the voidage calculation is wrong, since a VRR of one guarantees flat pressure by construction.", "That the reservoir is not the closed tank the balance assumes, which is information rather than an arithmetic error.", "That the pressure gauge has drifted.", "That the formation volume factors were evaluated at too low a pressure, which inflates the produced voidage and so understates the true VRR."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sometimes the calculation is wrong. More often the reservoir has terms the ledger structurally cannot see: an aquifer, a gas cap, a fault to a neighbour, or producers draining a part of the reservoir the injectors are not filling. That is the single most valuable thing a pressure survey can tell you, and a VRR of one guarantees flat pressure only for a genuinely closed tank on neutral conventions.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 12, 'Why is a shallow pressure trough dangerous to read from survey data?', '["Resolution, cadence and spatial variation all work against it: a 5 psi rounding hides a 7 psi feature, a six-monthly survey has at best two points inside a four-month event, and a normal injector-to-producer gradient is far larger than the feature itself.", "Because the trough is a model artefact that disappears once tracked formation volume factors are used.", "Because a trough in an average is always shallower than the trough in any individual well.", "Because the interpolation clamps flat outside the survey range, so any feature near the start of the record is flattened."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Ekene trough is 7.055251123068047 psi below the flood start, which is smaller than a typical gauge rounding, shorter than the survey interval, and far smaller than the spatial pressure variation across a field under injection. The trough is a genuine feature of the model track rather than an artefact, and the clamp affects only periods outside the survey range.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 13, 'attachPressure interpolates the surveys onto each period''s mid-month coordinate and clamps flat outside the survey range. What follows for 2023-01?', '["It reads a null, since no survey exists before it to interpolate from.", "It reads the model track value.", "It reads the first survey value exactly, because it sits before the first survey and is clamped.", "It reads the average of the first two surveys, since the clamp uses the local trend rather than the nearest value alone."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The clamp returns the nearest survey value rather than extrapolating, so every period before the first survey carries that survey''s pressure. That is itself a statement: it says the tool will not invent a trend it has not measured. Returning null is the alternative convention, and the engine deliberately does not fall back to the model track, which is a separate series.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 14, 'Ekene''s six surveys each read the END of the previous month, so every survey value is a row of the model track. Why does that matter for this teaching field?', '["It means the surveys can be used to fit the tank compressibility, since they contain no independent information.", "It makes the survey series a clean subsample of the track, so any difference between them is cadence rather than measurement error.", "It removes the need for interpolation.", "It means the survey dates are misaligned by one month, so the interpolated series should be shifted back before being compared with the track."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Because the surveys are exact samples of the track, the only thing separating the two series is how often you sample, which isolates the cadence lesson cleanly. On a real field the surveys would also carry gauge error and spatial effects and the two causes would be tangled. Interpolation is still needed for the 30 periods that carry no survey.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-pressure', 15, 'What is the correct status of the Ekene pressure track?', '["A measurement, since it is built from the metered production and injection volumes the field actually recorded.", "A calibration, since the six surveys were used to fit the tank compressibility and oil in place before the track was computed.", "An interpolation, since it is constructed by fitting a smooth curve through the six flood-era pressure surveys and extending it to monthly resolution.", "A model output, not a measurement."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It is computed from production volumes and assumed tank properties, and it is exactly as good as the assumption that Ekene is a single closed tank with the stated compressibilities. Nothing was fitted to the surveys: the compressibilities and the oil in place come from the earlier courses, which is what gives the comparison against the surveys any standing at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 1, 'buildFieldPeriods aggregates 216 per-well ledger rows into 36 field periods. What must happen before the aggregation?', '["Conversion to reservoir barrels, since periods are defined in voidage rather than in surface volumes.", "Well classification, because a well that ever injects is an injector even if it also produced.", "Allocation, so that each injector''s water is attributed to the producers it supports.", "Interpolation of the pressure track onto each period, since the formation volume factors depend on the period pressure."]'::jsonb, 1, 'classifyLedgerWells decides which rows contribute to production and which to injection, and injection wins: a well that ever injects is an injector for the whole record. Getting that wrong moves volumes to the wrong side of the ratio. Conversion happens inside the voidage calculation afterwards, allocation is a Professional tier input, and the ledger''s factors are frozen rather than period-dependent.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 2, 'A ledger row carries a date that does not begin with a four-digit year and a two-digit month. What does the engine do with it?', '["Assigns it to the nearest month for which a valid key can be inferred from the surrounding rows.", "Defaults it to the first period of the record, so that no volume is silently lost from the totals.", "Raises a validation error that stops the whole aggregation, since a ledger with an unparseable row cannot be trusted.", "Ignores it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'monthKeyOf returns null and the row is dropped: not defaulted, not assigned to a neighbour, and not fatal. That is a deliberate choice and it is exactly why the row counts matter, because a silently incomplete input produces a perfectly self-consistent wrong answer at every level above it. Check rows in against rows out before reading any value.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 3, 'Why does the lesson insist on checking the counts before the values?', '["Because a silently incomplete input produces a perfectly self-consistent wrong answer at every level above the ledger.", "Because the engine reports counts more precisely than it reports volumes.", "Because the count of rows determines the rolling window length the engine selects by default.", "Because the aggregation is order-dependent, so a missing row changes the floating-point result of every subsequent period."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing downstream can detect a row that never arrived: the periods aggregate cleanly, the ratios compute, the pressure track runs, and every number agrees with every other number. Rows in, rows out, and wells by name against the field''s well list is the only check that catches it. The aggregation is a sum and is not meaningfully order-dependent.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 4, 'In the daily surveillance engine, what happens to a repeated date and well combination?', '["It is summed, on the assumption that two rows represent two separate measurement periods within the same day.", "It is averaged.", "It is de-duplicated, keeping the first occurrence.", "It is retained in full and flagged in the data quality block, leaving the caller to decide whether to remove it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'cleanRows removes the duplicate and keeps the first, and reports the count it removed in its data quality block. On a deliberately dirty set of 15 rows with one exact duplicate, one blank well and one all-negative row it reports 1 duplicate removed, 1 row negatives-zeroed and 1 row dropped, with 15 in and 13 out. Summing would double-count and averaging would silently change a measurement.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 5, 'interpolateFvfTrack reproduces Ekene''s PVT line to a maximum relative error of 1.8266655911794395e-16. Why is it exact?', '["Because the underlying Bo is linear in pressure, and linear interpolation of a linear function is that function.", "Because the interpolator uses the analytic PVT line directly rather than the tabulated rows.", "Because the table is dense enough that the residual curvature falls below double precision.", "Because the pressures at which the track is evaluated all coincide with rows of the 200 psi table, so no interpolation actually takes place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ekene''s Bo(p) = 1.2(1 + 1.2e-5(3200 - p)) is a straight line, and a linear interpolant through points on a straight line reproduces it to machine precision. A real Bo curve is not linear and the same routine would then carry a genuine interpolation error, which is the point of noticing why the exactness happened. The engine takes only numbers and never correlations.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 6, 'interpolateFvfTrack clamps flat outside its table: 1000 psia returns the 1800 row and 9000 psia returns the 3400 row. What is the risk?', '["The clamp raises a warning that is easy to miss in a long warnings array.", "If the reservoir falls below the table, the factors stop changing at exactly the moment they should be changing fastest.", "The clamped factors are extrapolated linearly, so the error grows without bound outside the table.", "The clamp applies to the pressure but not to the solution gas ratio, so the two of them become inconsistent outside the table range."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Below the bubble point Bo curves sharply and Bg appears, which is precisely where a frozen end row is most wrong, and the clamp is silent. The rule is to check that your pressure track stays inside your table. Nothing is extrapolated, which is the safer of the two failure modes, and a null pressure returns null rather than a guessed factor set.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 7, 'The lesson names two ways a PVT table lies. What is the second, after clamping at the ends?', '["Stale sampling, since a PVT sample taken at the initial pressure no longer represents the flood-era fluid.", "Unit confusion between Mscf and scf in the solution gas column.", "Mixed sources: a table built partly from a lab report and partly from a correlation has a kink where they join.", "Interpolation order, since a linear interpolant between rows underestimates a convex Bo curve everywhere between the tabulated points."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Factors interpolated on either side of a lab-to-correlation join are not consistent with each other, and the discontinuity is invisible in the output. The Petrolord engine deliberately accepts only numbers and never correlations, so the correlation choice stays visible in the caller rather than hidden in the engine. Interpolation error on a curved Bo is real but is a precision issue rather than an inconsistency.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 8, 'Recomputing Ekene''s ledger with a pressure-tracked Bo gives 1.0349459620241488 against the frozen 1.034899536109. Why is the difference only 0.004486031110162436 percent?', '["Because the tracked run uses the same frozen factor for the first period, which dominates the cumulative.", "The flood-era pressure range is narrow at about 34.5 psi, and the convention was frozen near the middle of it so errors on either side partly cancel.", "Because the interpolation is exact.", "Because the cumulative ratio is dominated by the injection term, whose water factor does not vary with pressure at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two effects multiply: over a 34.5 psi span Bo moves by about 0.04 percent, and freezing at 2100 psia when the true pressures run 2089 to 2123 means the worst single-period error is about half the span and the errors partly cancel in the cumulative. Exact interpolation makes the tracked number trustworthy rather than small, and Bw is applied to the produced water too.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 9, 'The tracked cumulative VRR comes out slightly HIGHER than the frozen one. Why is that direction predictable in advance?', '["Most of the record sits above 2100 psia, where Bo is below 1.21584, so the tracked produced voidage is smaller and the ratio is larger.", "Because tracking always raises the ratio, since a pressure-dependent factor is never smaller than a frozen one.", "Because the tracked run includes gas voidage.", "Because the interpolated survey track sits above the model track for most of the record, and the ledger uses the survey track."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The pressure track runs from 2088.9530115439275 to 2123.4461408278908 psia, so most periods sit above the 2100 psia freezing point where Bo is smaller. A smaller Bo shrinks the denominator and raises the ratio. That sign is predictable from the pressure history alone and is worth predicting before computing, as a check on the machinery.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 10, 'What is the recommended resting state after testing the frozen convention against the tracked one?', '["Switch permanently to the tracked calculation, since it is strictly more accurate and now demonstrated to work.", "Remove the tracked path, since carrying untested code that is never run is a maintenance liability.", "Report both numbers side by side in every future summary, so that no reader has to take the convention on trust.", "Keep the machinery, use the convention."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tracked path exists, is tested, and can be run on demand, while the reported number stays the frozen one because it is simpler and now demonstrably adequate. That is the right resting state: the more complex option available and not used, for a stated reason. Reporting the 0.0045 percent result once ends the argument permanently.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 11, 'The frozen-versus-tracked result is 0.0045 percent for Ekene. Which conditions would break it?', '["A change in the allocation matrix, since pattern ledgers use different produced voidages.", "A longer record.", "Falling below the bubble point, a much wider pressure swing, or a convention frozen at the edge of the range.", "A change in the survey cadence, since the tracked run reads its pressures from the interpolated survey series."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The result is specific to a narrow, undersaturated pressure range with the freezing point near its middle. Below the bubble point Bo curves and Bg appears; a wide swing makes the linearisation matter; freezing at an edge removes the cancellation. Writing those conditions next to the result is what makes it portable, because the next person will have a different field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 12, 'What can the field ledger NOT see, and why does that not undermine it?', '["It cannot see where the water went, whether injectivity changed, the water arrival mechanism, or the sweep, and it remains the foundation because everything above it is built on its periods.", "It cannot see produced gas, which is why the ledger sets Bg to zero for Ekene.", "It cannot see the pressure, which is why the tier ends with a model track instead.", "It cannot see individual wells, since buildFieldPeriods sums across the field before any ratio is computed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those four blindnesses are exactly what the Professional and Expert tiers are for: allocation and pattern ledgers, Hall plots, Chan diagnostics, and the sweep calculation. None of them can be built without correct periods underneath, which is why the ledger is the foundation rather than a rough first pass. It reads per-well rows and does compute a pressure track.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 13, 'Ekene-4 loses thirty percent of its injectivity from 2025-01-01. Why is that invisible in the ledger?', '["The loss falls inside the 0.018379721651073933 percent gap between the volume and rate readings.", "The flood keeps hitting its volume target, so the rate data is unchanged and only the pressure moves.", "The ledger sums both injectors before computing a ratio.", "The injectivity index enters only into the allocation matrix, which the field ledger does not make any use of at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The injectivity model sets wellhead pressure as 2050 psia plus rate over the index, so a degraded index raises the pressure needed to deliver the same volume. The volume target is still met, so nothing in the volume record changes. Summing the injectors is real but even the per-well volumes are unchanged, which is why this is a pressure finding rather than a volume one.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 14, 'The Ekene surveillance rows are daily rates and the ledger rows are monthly volumes. buildFieldPeriods over the ledger rows reproduces the committed periods to 3.9364202640579826e-16. What does that establish?', '["That the surveillance rows were generated by dividing the ledger rows by the month length.", "That the aggregation is numerically stable.", "That the two files really are the same flood, and the difference between them is only the month-length multiply.", "That the committed period table can be discarded, since it can be regenerated from the rows whenever it is needed."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A maximum relative difference at the scale of double precision is agreement rather than approximation, so the two schemas describe the same 36 months of the same flood. It is a statement about the data and the aggregation together rather than about numerical stability alone, and it does not by itself say which file was generated from which.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-ledger-in-practice', 15, 'What does the Petrolord PVT interpolator return for a null pressure?', '["The factors at the nearest table row, which for a missing pressure is the first row of the table.", "The frozen ledger factor set, so that a period with no pressure still carries usable factors.", "An error, since a null pressure means the survey attachment failed and the caller must fix its inputs.", "Null."]'::jsonb, 3, 'It returns null rather than a guessed factor set, which pushes the decision back to the caller instead of quietly substituting something plausible. That is the same habit as the withheld pattern advice in the Professional tier and the producers excluded from the Chan table: a tool that says what it does not know is more useful than one that always returns a number.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'Capstone field 1 asks for the cumulative VRR. What is the check that catches most errors before you submit?', '["The answer should equal the latest instantaneous value of 1.05, since the field has been on target for years.", "The answer should equal the mean of the 36 monthly ratios.", "The answer should sit a little above one, since the field ran below target for five months and above it for thirty one.", "The answer should fall inside the 1.00 to 1.20 operator band, since that band was chosen to bracket the expected cumulative."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Five months below target and thirty one above it puts the cumulative modestly above one, and anything far from that says a factor or a series is wrong. It is a ratio of summed voidages rather than a mean of ratios, it is well below the latest instantaneous value because the early deficit is still in it, and the operator band applies to the instantaneous series.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'Capstone fields 2 and 3 ask for the produced and injected voidage separately, not just their ratio. Why is that not busywork?', '["If the ratio is wrong, the two sums tell you which side of it went wrong, and a produced voidage low by about eighteen percent is the signature of Bo defaulted to one.", "Because the grader tolerances on the two sums are tighter than on the ratio.", "Because the ratio alone cannot be reproduced without knowing the number of periods.", "Because the two sums are graded in reservoir barrels while the ratio is dimensionless, so they exercise different parts of the conversion."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A wrong ratio is one number with many possible causes; the two sums split it into two numbers with far fewer. The eighteen percent signature is specific and recognisable: 1.21584 against 1 is the difference. Both sums and the ratio come from the same conversion, and the period count is not needed to reproduce a ratio of totals.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Capstone field 4 asks for the fill-up month index. Which series do you use, and what is the consequence of the wrong choice?', '["The instantaneous series; the cumulative series never reaches 1.0 within the record.", "The cumulative series; the instantaneous series crosses 1.0 much earlier and puts you seven months out.", "The three-period rolling series, since that is the capstone panel setting.", "Either, since both cross 1.0 in the same period once the target profile has reached its 1.05 hold."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cumulative crosses at index 11 while the instantaneous crosses in month 5, so using the wrong one is a seven-month error that still looks like a plausible answer. Fill-up is by definition a statement about accumulated replacement rather than about a single month. The rolling window setting affects the panel display but not this field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'Capstone field 4''s crossing value is 1.0040017280735174. What does that imply about how you test for the crossing?', '["Test for strictly above 1.0, since a cumulative exactly at 1.0 has not yet replaced the voidage taken.", "Test against the operator band minimum of 1.00 rather than against 1.0 itself, since the two differ in the fourth decimal.", "Round the cumulative series to two decimals first, which removes the float tails that would otherwise trigger a spurious early crossing.", "Test for at or above 1.0, and do not round the value away."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The crossing is barely above one, so rounding to two decimals would report 1.00 and a strict inequality would miss it. The engine''s findFillUp tests at or above and reports index 11, label 2023-12, startedAbove false. Rounding away the margin is exactly the mistake, since there are no spurious float tails in a cumulative ratio of two large sums.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'Capstone field 5 counts months under the 1.00 to 1.20 band. Which series is flagged, and why does the other give a larger answer?', '["The instantaneous series, which takes five periods to reach 1.0, while the cumulative takes eleven and so would flag more months.", "The cumulative series, which is the one the operator band was defined against.", "The three-period rolling series, since a band is meaningless on a single noisy month.", "The instantaneous series, but the cumulative gives a smaller answer because it is smoothed and never falls as far below the band."]'::jsonb, 0, 'flagPeriods works on the instantaneous series, giving 4 under, 32 in band and 0 over on the 1.00 to 1.20 band, with the flagged months consecutive at the start. Flagging the cumulative against the same band gives a different and LARGER count, because the cumulative climbs more slowly. Which series a band applies to is the trap in this field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'Capstone field 6 asks for the January 2023 produced voidage. Why does the lesson say you can be right for a slightly wrong reason?', '["Because the produced water is zero that month, so the water factor never enters the arithmetic.", "Because the tolerance of 1 rb is loose relative to the gas term.", "With Bg = 0 the gas term vanishes whether or not you did the solution gas subtraction.", "Because the oil term alone reproduces the answer to within rounding, so a small free gas contribution would fall inside the tolerance anyway."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field exists to check that the subtraction was applied, and with Bg = 0 the gas term is zero either way, so the arithmetic forgives the omission. The lesson asks you to do the subtraction and watch it cancel, because on a field with free gas it will not. Adding the metered gas at a nonzero Bg would be high by a large margin, well outside any tolerance.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'The Associate tier''s three honest caveats close the story. Which is NOT one of them?', '["That six-monthly surveys put the apparent pressure minimum three months late and 2.427063793101752 psi too high.", "That the ledger cannot distinguish injected water from formation water at a producer.", "That the frozen factor convention is cheap here and might not be elsewhere.", "That everything reported is a field average, and a field average of a flood is an average over geometry."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The three caveats are the survey resolution limit, the narrowness of the pressure range that makes the convention cheap, and the fact that a field average hides its own halves. Telling injected from formation water is a real question and it is answered by a chemical analysis in the Expert tier, but it is not one of the tier''s stated caveats.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'What is the one fact that motivates the Professional tier?', '["That the field cumulative near 1.03 splits, once the record is divided by which producers each injector supports, into two elements near 1.20 and near 0.61.", "That the pressure trough was missed by the survey cadence, which per-well surveys would resolve.", "That the frozen factor convention moves the answer by 0.0045 percent.", "That the daily and monthly readings of the same record differ by 0.018379721651073933 percent, which grows with the size of the field."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither element is visible from anything in the Associate tier, and the field number is not an approximation to either: it is an average over a geometry the ledger deliberately does not model. The survey cadence and the two convention results are genuine Associate findings, and each is closed within the tier rather than motivating the next one.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'The Professional tier introduces an allocation matrix. What does the engine do with a pattern that has no allocation routed to its producers?', '["It falls back to an inverse-distance allocation computed from the mapped well coordinates.", "It assumes an even split across the pattern''s producers and flags the assumption.", "It returns a null result with no explanation, leaving the caller to discover the missing allocation from the inputs.", "It withholds the analysis and gives a reason, rather than assuming an even split."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The engine never invents a split: it returns withheld true with a reason naming the pattern, because even splits are never assumed. That is the same habit as the null the PVT interpolator returns for a null pressure. Falling back to distance would substitute a judgement the engine has no standing to make, and a bare null would hide why.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'Ekene''s allocation matrix leaves twelve percent of the injected water routed to no producer. How should that be treated?', '["As an error, since a validated allocation matrix must have every row summing to one.", "As a reportable quantity called out-of-zone injection, not as a data entry error.", "As rounding, since the row shares were rounded to the nearest 0.05.", "As unallocated water to be redistributed pro rata across the producers, so that the pattern ledgers account for the full injected volume."]'::jsonb, 1, 'validateAllocation treats a row summing above one as an ERROR and a row summing below one as a WARNING, precisely because out-of-zone injection is a real reportable quantity. Redistributing it would hide the very thing it measures. The shortfalls of 0.10 and 0.15 are design values rather than rounding artefacts.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'Ekene-4 loses thirty percent of its injectivity and the volume data shows nothing. What does the Professional tier use to find it, and what is the twist?', '["Hall plot analysis on injection pressure, and the step is visible under only one of two defensible pressure conventions.", "Cross-correlation of injector and producer rates, which resolves the change to within one month.", "The conservation audit, whose residual moves when an injector underperforms.", "Chan diagnostics on the producers that Ekene-4 supports, whose water oil ratio derivative changes slope at the same date."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The Hall integral against cumulative injection has a local slope of pressure over rate, which is the flow resistance, so an injectivity change shows there and nowhere else in the volume record. Run on absolute wellhead pressure the ratio reads 1.067 and raises no alert; run above the 2050 psia reference it reads about 1.43 and raises one. The conservation residual is exactly zero throughout.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'The Professional tier cross-correlates injector rates against producer rates to find which injector supports which producer. What does it find on Ekene?', '["That Ekene-2 supports Ekene-6 with a lag of three months, confirming the distance-based allocation.", "That the correlation is too weak to be significant.", "That the technique cannot separate the two injectors, for a structural rather than a statistical reason.", "That the lags rank the producers in the same order as their distances from each injector, which validates the matrix."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two injectors take a fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split of the same total, so their rate histories are proportional and every producer returns the same lag with a correlation identical to fifteen figures. No amount of data fixes that: the inputs carry no information that could distinguish them. Understanding why is worth more than the technique.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'What does the tier say to carry forward about stating conventions?', '["Name the rolling window, the target band and the series type used, since those are what a reader most often assumes wrongly.", "Name the pressure the factors came from, whether gas voidage is counted, and whether the solution gas subtraction fired.", "Name the engine version and the fixture commit.", "Name the well classification rule and the duplicate handling policy, since those decide which rows entered the aggregation at all."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Those three clauses are what makes a VRR reproducible, and none of them is discoverable from the answer. The window, the band and the series type matter too but they belong in the chart label rather than beside the number, and the classification and duplicate rules are checked through the row counts rather than quoted with the value.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'Before reading any value out of a flood ledger, what should be verified first?', '["Recompute every value at two different rolling windows, so that a window-sensitive result is caught before it is reported.", "Report every value to the full precision the engine returns, so that a near-miss can be told apart from a wrong method.", "Cross-check the ledger against the daily surveillance engine, since the two use different code paths and should agree to within rounding.", "Check the counts before the values."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Rows in, rows out, and wells by name against the field''s well list. A silently incomplete input produces a perfectly self-consistent wrong answer at every level above the ledger, and nothing downstream can detect it. The two engines do agree to 0.018379721651073933 percent, but that comparison answers a different question from whether all the rows arrived.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'The grader compares each capstone field against an oracle from the engines on the committed fixture. If you believe your answer is right and it is marked wrong, what is it almost always?', '["A tolerance set too tightly for a value that carries genuine floating point spread.", "A difference between the panel and the engine.", "The wrong series, the wrong band, a missing formation volume factor, or an off-by-one in the index.", "An engine version mismatch, since the oracle was produced from a fixture commit that may predate the one the panel loads."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing is graded on a rounded value and nothing is graded on a number you cannot derive from the data you were given, so the failure is in the derivation rather than in the grading. Those four cover almost every case. The panel and the engine share the same computation, and the fixture is committed alongside both.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'A flood holds a cumulative VRR of one for a decade while cycling water through a thief zone and recovering almost nothing extra. What does that show?', '["That the VRR was computed on the wrong formation volume factors, since a real VRR of one implies displacement.", "That the thief zone must be outside the pattern.", "That voidage replacement measures the pressure job and says nothing about the displacement job.", "That the cumulative form is the wrong one to quote, and an instantaneous VRR would have revealed the recycling within a month or two."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two jobs are separate and are measured by different numbers. A flood can replace every reservoir barrel it removes, hold pressure perfectly, and still leave most of the oil behind if the water finds a fast path. No form of the VRR detects that, because none of them knows where the water went.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Ekene-2 found the top of the sand at 1565 m and Ekene-4 at 1590 m, against a mapped oil water contact at 1560 m. What does that make them?', '["Wet wells, already completed in the right rock and cheap to convert to injection.", "Structurally high wells, which is why they were chosen to inject downdip of the producers.", "Deviated wells, whose measured depths exceed true vertical depth.", "Wells that penetrated a separate deeper compartment, which is why the allocation matrix leaves twelve percent of their water out of zone."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both depths sit below the 1560 m contact, so both wells found water rather than oil. A wet well in a sand you are about to flood will never produce oil and converting it costs a pump and a line rather than a well, which is how flood patterns are usually chosen in practice.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'At the flood start Ekene sat at 2096.0082626669955 psia against a bubble point of 2000 psia. What would have happened had the flood been much later?', '["The oil formation volume factor would have risen, increasing the reservoir volume each produced barrel occupied.", "The tank compressibility would have fallen.", "The reservoir would have crossed into the region where the closed-form pressure inversion no longer has a solution, so the ledger could not be computed at all.", "Gas would have come out of solution, raising the oil viscosity and taking flow capacity from the oil."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Below the bubble point free gas appears in the rock: the oil viscosity rises, gas competes for flow capacity, and the well starts producing the field''s energy instead of its oil. Bo falls rather than rises below the bubble point, the total compressibility rises sharply once gas appears, and the inversion is simply no longer the right model rather than unsolvable.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Ekene''s injection split sends 0.6 of each month''s volume to Ekene-2 and 0.4 to Ekene-4. What does the Professional tier''s allocation matrix add on top of that?', '["A correction for the injectivity loss at Ekene-4, which shifts the split after 2025-01-01.", "Where each injector''s water goes underground, which producers it supports and in what shares.", "A conversion of the split from surface to reservoir barrels.", "A time lag between injection at the wellhead and arrival at the producer, derived from the cross-correlation of the two rate series."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The split is a surface fact about which pump got the water. The allocation is a subsurface judgement about which producers that water supports, informed by distance or streamline modelling, and it is the input the engine refuses to invent. The split is fixed for the whole record and both quantities are surface volumes.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'The surveillance file and the ledger file describe the same Ekene flood. What is the only difference between them?', '["One holds daily rates and the other monthly volumes, related by the number of days in the month.", "One is per well and the other is per field period, so the ledger has already been aggregated.", "One is at surface conditions and the other at reservoir conditions.", "One covers the flood era only while the other extends back through the primary depletion period from 2020-01-01."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both files are one row per well per month over the same 36 months, both are surface quantities, and volume equals rate times days in month. Running buildFieldPeriods over the 216 ledger rows reproduces the committed field periods to a maximum relative difference of 3.9364202640579826e-16.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Treating Ekene''s 36 monthly rows as equal-weight samples gives a cumulative VRR of 1.034709324454895 against the volume-weighted 1.034899536109. When would that error be much larger?', '["On a field with more than 36 periods, since the error accumulates with record length.", "On a field with a higher water cut.", "On a field whose injection is set by a facility constraint rather than computed from the produced voidage.", "On a field with a wider pressure range, since the formation volume factors would then vary enough for the month weighting to matter."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The error is worth however much the ratio you care about correlates with month length. On Ekene the injection was computed from the production each month so the ratio barely varies and the error is 0.018 percent; where injection is set by a fixed facility rate the monthly ratio swings with month length directly and the same mistake can be worth several percent.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'Why does the engine derive a row''s month from the leading characters of the date string rather than by parsing a date?', '["Because string comparison is faster than date parsing over 216 rows.", "Because a timezone shift can move a row dated the first of the month into the previous month, silently.", "Because the ledger dates carry no day component.", "Because some rows carry month-only dates like 2023-05, which a date parser would reject rather than accept as the first of that month."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Parsing to a date object and asking for its month works until a timezone is involved, and then a volume moves between periods with nothing raised. monthKeyOf takes ''2023-05-17'' and ''2023-05'' both to ''2023-05'' and returns null for ''May 2023'', so it handles both shapes without ever constructing a calendar date.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Ekene''s Bo of 1.21584 means what, precisely?', '["One reservoir barrel of oil yields 1.21584 stock tank barrels once the gas has come out of it at the surface.", "The oil expands by 21.584 percent on its way to the tank.", "The oil is 21.584 percent gas by volume at reservoir conditions, which is the fraction that leaves at the separator.", "One stock tank barrel of oil occupied 1.21584 barrels of pore space in the reservoir."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Bo is reservoir barrels over stock tank barrels, so it converts a tank volume up to the space that same parcel occupied underground. The oil SHRINKS on the way up rather than expanding, and the factor is not a gas volume fraction: it lumps the loss of dissolved gas and the thermal contraction together.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'How does Bg differ in character from Bo and Bw?', '["It is dimensionless, since gas volumes are already reported at reservoir conditions.", "It converts gas measured at standard conditions into reservoir space, and it moves strongly with pressure because gas is very compressible.", "It is always less than one.", "It applies to the total produced gas rather than to a parcel, so it cannot be used to convert an individual well''s gas stream."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bg is in reservoir barrels per thousand standard cubic feet and it changes sharply with pressure, unlike the two liquid factors which move only slightly. Ekene''s ledger sets it to zero because there is no free gas for it to multiply, which is a statement about the reservoir rather than about the factor.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Why must the solution gas be subtracted from the metered gas before any gas voidage is counted?', '["Because Bo already includes the space that dissolved gas occupied, so counting the metered gas again charges the reservoir twice for the same molecules.", "Because the gas meter reads at standard conditions while the oil meter reads at tank conditions.", "Because solution gas is re-injected.", "Because the free gas term uses a different formation volume factor from the solution gas, so the two must be separated before either is converted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bo is defined at reservoir conditions where the gas was still in solution, so the oil term already carries that volume. Only gas that came out of the rock as free gas rather than out of the oil belongs in the voidage. Gfree = max(0, Gp - Rs Np / 1000), with the thousand converting scf to Mscf.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'A VRR is quoted with no further qualification. Which three questions make it reproducible?', '["Which wells were included, over what date range, and at what rolling window.", "Which engine version, which fixture, and which tolerance.", "At what pressure the factors were evaluated, whether gas voidage is counted, and whether the solution gas subtraction was applied.", "Whether the series is instantaneous or cumulative, whether the band is generic or operator-specific, and whether the index is zero-based."]'::jsonb, 2, 'None of the three is discoverable from the answer, and each can move it materially: the pressure sets Bo, a nonzero Bg adds a whole term, and omitting the subtraction on a field with gas double counts. The other sets are real reporting questions but they are visible in a chart label or an index convention rather than hidden inside the number.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'Where does produced water sit in the voidage equation, and what follows for a maturing flood?', '["In the numerator, so a rising water cut raises the VRR and masks a falling injection rate.", "In neither, since produced water is recycled.", "In the denominator, but weighted by the fraction that is injected water rather than formation water, since only the latter represents new voidage.", "In the denominator, so a rising water cut forces injection up to hold a target."]'::jsonb, 3, 'VRR is a voidage balance rather than a water balance: produced water left the pore space, so it belongs with the oil in the denominator, and only injected fluids appear on top. That is why mature waterfloods inject enormous volumes for modest oil, most of it replacing the voidage created by the water they produced last month.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Handing the surveillance engine capitalised factor keys makes it default Bo to 1 and report a cumulative VRR of 1.2263567352896008. Why does that pass unnoticed?', '["The value falls inside the generic over-injection band, so no alert fires.", "No error, warning or null is produced, and a flood reading 1.23 looks like a healthy over-injected flood.", "The inflation factor of 1.185218598407499 is small enough to be mistaken for a convention difference.", "The engine reports the defaulted factors in its output block, but that block is not rendered on the standard dashboard."]'::jsonb, 1, 'An implausible number gets checked and a plausible wrong one gets reported, which is why the silence rather than the size is the danger. 1.23 sits well ABOVE the 1.1 over-injection band and would be classified as over-injection, and a nineteen percent inflation is far too large to read as a convention difference.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Ekene''s PVT line is Bo(p) = 1.2(1 + 1.2e-5(3200 - p)). What does it give at the initial 3200 psia, and what is that value called?', '["1.2, which is Boi, the factor the volumetric booking used and the reference the tank equation measures expansion from.", "1.2, which is Bob, the factor at the bubble point, below which the line no longer applies.", "1.21584, since 3200 psia is the flood-era reference pressure.", "1.0, since the line is normalised so that the factor is unity at the initial pressure and grows as pressure falls."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At p = 3200 the bracket is exactly 1 and the line returns 1.2. That is Boi at the INITIAL pressure, not the bubble point value, and the difference between it and the ledger''s frozen 1.21584 at 2100 psia is exactly what puts pressure break-even at 0.9869719699960521 rather than 1.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'Which VRR form has the longest memory, and what is the consequence?', '["The rolling at a long window, which retains every period once the window exceeds the record length.", "The instantaneous, since it carries the design target profile.", "All three equally, since each is computed from the same running totals and simply reported over a different span.", "The cumulative, so a bad first year is still in the number three years later."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The cumulative runs from the start of the record to the current period, so nothing ever leaves it, which is either a feature or an annoyance depending on the question. A long rolling window approaches the cumulative but only reproduces it once the window is at least as long as the record, and the instantaneous carries no memory at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'Why is the rolling VRR computed as a ratio of summed voidages rather than as a mean of the monthly ratios?', '["So that the series remains defined in months where no oil was produced, which a mean of ratios would not be.", "So that the rolling series reduces to the cumulative at long windows.", "So that each month is weighted by the voidage it actually created.", "So that the series is insensitive to the number of days in each month, which a mean of ratios would inherit from the rate conversion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A month creating twice the voidage should count twice, and only the ratio of sums does that. On a field where produced voidage varies month to month the two forms give genuinely different numbers. Both forms reduce to the instantaneous at a window of one, and month-length weighting is handled before either is computed.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Ekene''s instantaneous series prints 0.9299999999999999 where the design says 0.93. What is the correct response?', '["Recognise it as the exact binary result of 0.85 + 0.04m and leave it alone.", "Round the series to two decimals before reporting, so that the design values are recovered.", "Investigate, since a designed fixture should reproduce its targets exactly.", "Recompute with a higher-precision accumulator, since the tail indicates that the monthly sums were accumulated in an unstable order."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Neither 0.04 nor its multiples are exactly representable in binary, so the sixteenth digit moves. A number that differs from a round value in the sixteenth digit IS that round value. Rounding the series would also round away the genuinely small margin at the fill-up crossing, where 1.0040017280735174 matters.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'Ekene under-injects deliberately for five months. Which is NOT one of the standard justifications?', '["A new water plant does not reach its design rate on day one while pumps and filters are being proved.", "Injected water must first fill the free gas saturation, which produces no oil response.", "An injectivity index is an estimate until a well has been injected into.", "The reservoir still had margin above its bubble point and did not yet need the support."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fill-up against a free gas saturation is a real mechanism and it is why many floods show no early response, but Ekene never went below its bubble point so it has no free gas at all. The other three are the standard commissioning, measurement and urgency arguments, and all three apply here.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'A twelve-period rolling window reads 0.9775428385647656 in a month when the field has been on its 1.05 target for three months. What is the general lesson?', '["A longer window is more accurate, so the twelve-period reading should be preferred for reporting.", "The partial-window convention corrupts long windows early in a record.", "A longer window is more stable, not more accurate, and the window must match the question.", "Trailing windows should be centred rather than trailing, which removes the lag at the cost of not being computable for the most recent periods."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reading is a perfectly accurate statement about the last twelve months, four of which were below target, and a badly misleading answer to how the flood is doing now. Accuracy is not the axis. The partial-window convention is a separate and defensible choice, and a centred window cannot be computed for the current period at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'classifyVRR flags below 0.9 and above 1.1, while Ekene''s operator band is 1.00 to 1.20. What is the relationship?', '["The first is the reservoir-condition band and the second the surface-condition equivalent, related by the formation volume factors.", "The second replaces the first once an operator band is configured, so only one set of flags is ever produced.", "The first applies to the instantaneous series and the second to the cumulative, which is why they have different widths.", "The first is generic reservoir engineering and the second is a field-specific input you supply."]'::jsonb, 3, 'They are separate and both are reported: the generic bands are untouched reservoir engineering and the operator band is the plan you are being held to. Confusing them produces a dashboard that flags the plan as a problem. Both are dimensionless ratios and both are applied to the instantaneous series.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'findFillUp on the Ekene field series returns index 11, label 2023-12, startedAbove false. What would startedAbove true have meant?', '["That the cumulative VRR was already at or above 1.0 in the first period, so the crossing is not in the record.", "That the field started the record above its operator target band rather than below it.", "That the instantaneous series crossed 1.0 before the cumulative did.", "That the fill-up occurred before the flood started, during the primary depletion period covered by the earlier courses."]'::jsonb, 0, 'startedAbove distinguishes a genuine crossing inside the record from a series that was already above the threshold when the record began, which is an honest answer to a question the data cannot otherwise settle. The Professional tier''s north element returns exactly that, at index 0, while the south element returns null because it never reaches 1 at all.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'The material balance behind voidage replacement assumes what about the reservoir?', '["That the pressure is uniform across the field and equal to the value a single survey would read.", "That the oil compressibility is negligible.", "That it is a closed container of nearly incompressible fluid and rock, with no aquifer and no gas cap.", "That injection and production are balanced within each period, which is what makes the cumulative form well defined."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Closure is the load-bearing assumption: an aquifer, an expanding gas cap or a fault to a neighbour all add terms the ledger cannot see. The pressure is explicitly an average that no gauge reads exactly, the oil compressibility is central rather than negligible, and the balance holds over any span rather than requiring period-by-period equality.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Ekene''s total effective compressibility is 1.976923076923077e-5 per psi. Why is its smallness the reason waterflooding works?', '["Because a small compressibility means the reservoir can absorb large injected volumes without fracturing.", "Because a small compressibility means very little volume is released per psi, so unreplaced voidage is expensive and replacing it is correspondingly effective.", "Because it makes the pressure insensitive to production.", "Because it keeps the oil formation volume factor nearly constant over the flood-era pressure range, which is what allows the ledger to freeze it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'N Boi times that compressibility is about 288 reservoir barrels of expansion per psi, so 288 rb of unreplaced voidage costs a psi. That is a losing arithmetic to let run and a very favourable one to reverse. Pressure is highly sensitive to production, not insensitive, and fracture pressure is a near-well constraint.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'In the flood-era inversion, what is W = (Wp - Wi) Bw / Boi?', '["The water saturation change over the period, converted to a volume by the pore volume.", "The water influx from the aquifer, which the closed-tank form sets to zero.", "The injected water volume alone, since produced water is already carried inside the produced voidage on the other side of the balance.", "Net water withdrawal expressed on the same basis as Np."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dividing by Boi puts the net water term into the same units as the cumulative oil so the two can be added in the numerator. Setting W to zero recovers exactly the depletion-era inversion, which is why the flood-era track continues the depletion track with no seam. There is no aquifer term in this model, and produced water is explicitly present in W.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'Ekene''s pressure break-even is 0.9869719699960521. What single ratio is that?', '["Bw over Bo, which converts the injected water volume into the equivalent oil voidage.", "Boi over Bo: 1.2 at the initial 3200 psia over the frozen 1.21584 at 2100 psia.", "The ratio of the oil compressibility to the total effective compressibility.", "The ratio of the flood-start pressure to the initial pressure, which measures how much of the expansion budget had been spent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The derivation cancels the monthly oil increment and leaves Boi/Bo exactly. The ledger books a produced barrel at flood-era conditions while the tank measures expansion from its initial state, and those references differ by 1100 psi of oil expansion, which is 1.3 percent of volume.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'Ekene''s pressure trough sits at month index 3 rather than index 4, where the target profile first exceeds 1.0. Why does that position matter?', '["It is a directly observable consequence of the break-even being below one, and it would be easy to mistake for a small error in the data.", "It shows the tank responds to injection with a one-month lag.", "It shows the survey cadence resolved the trough correctly.", "It shows the target profile is indexed from one while the pressure track is indexed from zero, so the two series are offset by a period."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The month with a target of 0.97 was already above the 0.9869719699960521 break-even, so pressure had started rising by then. A convention choice showing up as the position of a minimum is exactly the kind of thing that gets written off as noise. The surveys in fact put the apparent minimum three months late.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'The six-monthly surveys put Ekene''s apparent pressure minimum at 2091.380075337029 psia in 2023-07. How wrong is that, and why is it not fixable?', '["Three months early and 2.427063793101752 psi low, because the interpolation clamps flat before the first survey.", "Wrong only in timing, since the value itself is exact.", "Three months late and 2.427063793101752 psi high, because a six-monthly cadence cannot resolve a two-month event.", "Wrong by the gauge resolution of 5 psi, which is the dominant error and would be removed by a more precise instrument."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The true trough is 2088.9530115439275 psia at 2023-04, and the linear segment between the February and August surveys is monotone while the truth is V-shaped. There is simply no data inside the feature, so no interpolation scheme recovers it. The surveys are exact samples of the track, so gauge resolution is not the issue here.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'What gives the Ekene pressure track any standing at all?', '["That it is tested against six real pressure surveys, since it is otherwise a model output resting on the closed-tank assumption.", "That it reproduces the RC2 fixture''s flood-start pressure exactly, which validates the tank properties.", "That it is computed from metered volumes.", "That the survey values were used to fit the tank compressibility and the oil in place, so the track passes through them by construction."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The track is computed from production volumes and assumed tank properties and is exactly as good as the assumption that Ekene is a single closed tank. Agreeing with RC2 at the flood start shows the two courses run the same closed form rather than that the form is right. Nothing was fitted to the surveys, which is what makes the comparison a test.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'classifyLedgerWells is run before any aggregation. What is its rule when a well both produced and injected?', '["Production wins, since a well that ever produced oil is booked as a producer for reserves purposes.", "The well is split by date, producing rows before its conversion and injecting rows after it.", "The well is excluded from both sides and reported in the data quality block, since a mixed-role well cannot be assigned unambiguously.", "Injection wins: it is an injector for the whole record."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A well that ever injects is an injector even if it also produced, and getting that wrong moves volumes to the wrong side of the ratio. Ekene''s classification returns injectors Ekene-2 and Ekene-4 and producers Ekene-1, Ekene-3, Ekene-5 and Ekene-6, which matches the flood history.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'cleanRows is given 15 rows containing one exact duplicate, one blank well and one all-negative row. What comes out?', '["12 rows, since all three problem rows are removed.", "13 rows: the duplicate removed, the negatives zeroed in place, and the blank-well row dropped.", "15 rows, all retained and flagged.", "14 rows, since the duplicate is removed and the all-negative row is zeroed, while the blank-well row is assigned to an unnamed well and kept for the totals."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The three problems get three different treatments: the duplicate is dropped keeping the first occurrence, the negatives are zeroed and the row survives, and the row with no well name cannot be classified so it is dropped. Fifteen in, thirteen out, with each count reported in the data quality block.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'interpolateFvfTrack reproduces Ekene''s Bo line to 1.8266655911794395e-16. What should you NOT conclude?', '["That linear interpolation of a linear function returns that function.", "That the Ekene PVT table was evaluated on a straight line.", "That the interpolator will be similarly exact on a real PVT curve.", "That the tracked-versus-frozen comparison can be trusted, since the interpolation contributes no error of its own to it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The exactness follows entirely from Ekene''s Bo being linear in pressure. A real Bo curve is not linear and the same routine would then carry a genuine interpolation error, which is the whole point of noticing WHY the exactness happened rather than just recording it.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'Your reservoir pressure falls below the bottom of your PVT table. What does interpolateFvfTrack do, and why is that the dangerous case?', '["It returns the lowest row''s factors silently, so the factors stop changing at exactly the moment they should be changing fastest.", "It extrapolates the last two rows linearly, so the error grows steadily as you move further below the table.", "It returns null for every period below the table.", "It raises a warning and continues with the lowest row, which is safe provided the warnings array is inspected."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The clamp is flat and silent: 1000 psia returns the 1800 row. Below the bubble point Bo curves sharply and Bg appears, which is precisely where a frozen end row is most wrong. Null is what a null PRESSURE returns, which is a different case, and no warning is raised for the clamp.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'The tracked-Bo cumulative VRR is 1.0349459620241488 against the frozen 1.034899536109. Which conditions would make that difference matter?', '["A longer record, more periods, or a higher water cut.", "Falling below the bubble point, a much wider pressure swing, or a convention frozen at the edge of the pressure range.", "A different rolling window or target band.", "A change in the allocation matrix or the pattern definitions, since pattern ledgers use different produced voidages and so different pressures."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 0.0045 percent result is specific to a narrow undersaturated range with the freezing point near its middle, where the errors on either side partly cancel. Below the bubble point Bo curves and Bg appears, a wide swing breaks the linearisation, and freezing at an edge removes the cancellation. Writing those conditions beside the result is what makes it portable.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'The tracked cumulative VRR comes out higher than the frozen one. What lets you predict that sign before computing?', '["Tracking always raises a VRR, since a variable factor is never below its frozen value.", "The water factor is tracked too, and Bw rises with pressure.", "Most of the record sits above the 2100 psia freezing point, where Bo is smaller, so the produced voidage shrinks.", "The tracked run reads the interpolated survey series, which sits above the model track for most of the record."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The track runs from 2088.9530115439275 to 2123.4461408278908 psia against a freezing point of 2100, so most periods sit above it where Bo is below 1.21584. A smaller denominator gives a larger ratio. Predicting the sign before computing is a check on the machinery rather than a general rule about tracking.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'What does the field ledger structurally fail to see?', '["Where the water went, whether injectivity changed, the water arrival mechanism, and the sweep.", "The produced gas, the pressure and the well classification.", "The month lengths and the formation volume factors.", "The cumulative totals, which have to be reconstructed from the daily surveillance engine because the ledger holds only per-period values."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Those four blindnesses are exactly what the Professional and Expert tiers are built on: allocation and pattern ledgers, Hall plots, Chan diagnostics and the sweep calculation. None of them can be built without correct periods underneath, which is why the ledger remains the foundation rather than a rough first pass.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Capstone field 5 counts months under the operator band. Flagging the cumulative series instead of the instantaneous gives what?', '["A smaller count, because the cumulative is smoothed and never falls as far below the band.", "The same count, since both series start below the band and rise through it once.", "A larger count, because the cumulative takes eleven periods to reach 1.0 while the instantaneous takes five.", "An undefined count, since the operator band is only meaningful against a series that can fall as well as rise."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The instantaneous series rises through 1.0 in month five, giving 4 months under the 1.00 to 1.20 band. The cumulative climbs more slowly and does not reach 1.0 until index 11, so flagging it against the same band flags more months. Which series a band applies to is the trap in this field.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Capstone field 6 is the January 2023 produced voidage. What is it testing?', '["That the water formation volume factor was applied, since January carries the largest produced water volume of the record.", "That the reader used the instantaneous rather than the cumulative series for a single month.", "That the reader converted the January daily rates to a monthly volume using 31 days rather than the mean month of 30.4375.", "That the solution gas subtraction was applied."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The field asks you to compute Rs Np / 1000, confirm it equals Gp so the free gas is zero, and then take the oil term alone. January has no produced water at all, and the capstone is given the monthly volumes directly rather than the rates.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'A capstone produced voidage that is low by about eighteen percent is the signature of what?', '["The solution gas subtraction omitted, which removes the gas term from the produced side.", "Bo defaulted to one instead of 1.21584.", "The produced water omitted from the denominator.", "The cumulative taken over 30 periods instead of 36, which drops the last six months of the record."]'::jsonb, 1, '1.21584 against 1 is about eighteen percent, and the two sums are reported separately precisely so that a wrong ratio can be attributed to a side. Omitting the subtraction would make the produced voidage too HIGH on a field with gas and makes no difference at all on Ekene, where Bg is zero.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'Which of the Associate tier''s three honest caveats points directly at the next tier?', '["That six-monthly surveys cannot resolve the pressure trough, which per-pattern surveys would fix.", "That the frozen factor convention is cheap on Ekene and might not be elsewhere.", "That the daily and monthly readings of the same record differ by 0.018379721651073933 percent.", "That everything reported is a field average, and an average of a flood is an average over geometry."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The field average hiding its own halves is the caveat the Professional tier exists to answer, by splitting the record by which producers each injector supports. The survey resolution and the convention result are closed within the Associate tier, and the daily-versus-monthly gap is a reporting distinction rather than a caveat about geometry.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Cross-correlating Ekene''s injector rates against its producer rates cannot say which injector supports which producer. Why not?', '["The two injectors take a fixed 0.6 to 0.4 split of the same total, so their histories are proportional and every producer returns the same lag.", "The monthly sampling is too coarse relative to the transit times, which are of order weeks.", "The correlation is computed on raw rather than detrended series.", "The record is too short: 36 periods cannot resolve a lag of six months at any useful confidence."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is a structural limitation rather than a statistical one: the inputs carry no information that could separate two proportional series, and the correlations come out identical to fifteen figures. The engine does detrend by first differences, and no amount of extra data or finer sampling would help.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'What does the Professional tier''s conservation audit establish about Ekene''s allocation?', '["That the allocation shares were derived correctly from the mapped well distances.", "That no injected water left the pattern.", "That the allocated volumes plus the out-of-zone volume account for the total injection with a residual of exactly zero.", "That the out-of-zone fraction of twelve percent is within the tolerance the engine allows before raising an error on the matrix."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The audit is a bookkeeping check: every injected barrel is either allocated to a producer or booked as out-of-zone, and the residual is exactly zero rather than 1e-9. It says nothing about whether the shares are RIGHT, which is a judgement, and the twelve percent out of zone is precisely water that did leave the pattern.'),
  ('waterflood', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which pair of habits does the Associate tier ask you to carry into every later tier?', '["Report to full precision, and always prefer the cumulative form over the instantaneous.", "Use the longest available rolling window, and flag against the generic bands rather than the operator band.", "Reconcile the ledger against the daily engine every period, and freeze the formation volume factors at the midpoint of the observed pressure range.", "State your conventions with your number, and check the counts before the values."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Three clauses on the conventions, every time, because none of them is discoverable from the answer. And rows in, rows out, wells by name, because a silently incomplete input produces a perfectly self-consistent wrong answer at every level above the ledger. The other options each recommend one defensible practice alongside a bad one.');
